1 DELETE

   Deletes files from a mounted volume, giving their space back.

   Format

     DELETE filespec[,...]

   DELETE/SYMBOL deletes a DCL symbol instead (see DELETE /SYMBOL).

2 Parameter

 filespec[,...]

   The files to delete.  Each must have a version: a number (;3), every
   version (;*), or the newest, written ;0 or a semicolon alone
   (FOO.TXT;).  Wildcards are allowed in the name, type, and version.

   Specifications are separated by commas or plus signs.  A part left
   out of the first is the default device and directory's (see SET
   DEFAULT), and a part left out of a later one, but its version, is the
   one before it's: DELETE A.TXT;1,;2,B;1 deletes A.TXT;1, A.TXT;2, and
   B.TXT;1.

2 Description

   DELETE needs a version so that the newest version isn't deleted by
   mistake: unlike DIRECTORY, TYPE, and COPY, it never takes a missing
   version to mean the newest.

   It says nothing of the files it deletes unless /LOG is given.  A
   specification that finds no file is a warning, and the others are
   still deleted:

     %DELETE-W-SEARCHFAIL, error searching for DUA0:[WORK]X.TMP;*
     -RMS-E-FNF, file not found

2 Qualifiers
/LOG

   Shows each file deleted, and the blocks it had:

     %DELETE-I-FILDEL, DUA0:[WORK]FOO.TXT;1 deleted (3 blocks)

2 Examples

   1.$ DELETE FOO.TXT;1

     Deletes version 1 of FOO.TXT.

   2.$ DELETE FOO.TXT;

     Deletes the newest version of FOO.TXT.

   3.$ DELETE/LOG A.LIS;*,B;*,C.OBJ;*

     Deletes every version of A.LIS, B.LIS, and C.OBJ, showing each.

2 /SYMBOL

   Deletes a DCL symbol, or every symbol in a table.

   Format

     DELETE/SYMBOL [symbol-name]

3 Parameter

 symbol-name

   The symbol to delete, in any abbreviation the symbol allows (see
   SYMBOL).  It is needed unless /ALL is given.

3 Qualifiers
/ALL

   Deletes every symbol in the table, instead of one.

/GLOBAL

   Deletes from the global symbol table.

/LOCAL

   Deletes from the current command level's local symbol table.  This is
   the default.

3 Examples

   1.$ DELETE/SYMBOL COUNT

     Deletes the local symbol COUNT.

   2.$ DELETE/SYMBOL/GLOBAL/ALL

     Deletes every global symbol.
